Hello,

I need to draw many disjoint vertical which as far as I can tell isn't a built in function (a little ridiculous, but that's another topic).

I've resorted to using layer_paths() many times, each time with two points. However, iteration seems not to be an option because only the last layer_paths() in the %>% chain is displaying. I've even tried writing a recursive function in an attempt to preserve %>% chain:

# ...
# Other ggvis() %>% chain calls
# ...

# pl is the ggvis() object
# pos_x is a numeric vector containing x values
# pos_y is a numeric vector containing y values
# n controls the base case
add_stick <- function(pl, pos_x, pos_y, n){
      tmp_df = data.frame(x=c(pos_x[n], pos_x[n]),
                          y=c(pos_y[n], 0))
      if(n == 1){
        return(layer_paths(~x, ~y, data = tmp_df))
      } else {
        return(layer_paths(~x, ~y, data = tmp_df) %>% add_stick(pl, pos_x, pos_y, n - 1))
      }
    }
# all_x and all_y are vectors of same length
pl %>% add_stick(all_x, all_y, length(all_x))

I'm not necessarily dedicated to this recursive function (especially since it doesn't actually work), but I don't know how else to accomplish this (seemingly) simple task of drawing disjoint vertical lines.

Thanks in advance,
Dominic

